  <title>Write Code Questions - Parsons for Select</title>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode1q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode1q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
       <p>Create a function called <c>cur_stud_link(soup)</c> that takes a soup object and returns the URL that links to Current Students in the Navigation Bar.
                        The soup object is created from the file <c>UMich-8-16-2026.html</c> which is a saved version of <q>https://umich.edu/</q> from 2026. 
                        Click on the following link to view the source:<url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20260808043645/https://umich.edu/">link</url>.

                         For example, <c>cur_stud_link(soup)</c> should return <c>'https://umich.edu/web/20260808043645/https://umich.edu/current-students/'</c>.
                        </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="8">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="10">
        <cline>def cur_stud_link(soup):</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
         <choice>
          <cline>    text = soup.find_all('a', string="Current Students")  </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    text = soup.find('a', string="Current Students")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="5">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    info = text.get('href')</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    info = text.get(href) </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>    all_info = "https://umich.edu" + info</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="2">
        <cline>    return all_info</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode2qv2_mu" label="bsoup_writecode2qv2_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
       <p>Create a function called <c>headings(soup)</c> that takes a soup object and returns a list of the text from all <c>h2</c> tags in the soup.
                    The soup object is created from the file <c>MOOC-Wikipedia-8-20-2026.html</c> which is a saved version of <q>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massive_open_online_course</q> from 2026. 
      Click on the 
      following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20260807063804/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massive_open_online_course">link</url>

      For example, <c>headings(soup)</c> should return a list of 14 headings starting with <c>['Contents', 'History', 'Equity and access', ...]</c>. 
    </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="4">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>def headings(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    out_list = []</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="2">
        <choice>
        <cline>    h_list = soup.find("h2")   </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
        <cline>    h_list = soup.find_all("h2")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>    for h_tag in h_list:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="8">
        <choice>
          <cline>        out_list.append(h_tag) </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        out_list.append(h_tag.text)</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
          <cline>    return out_list</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode3q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode3q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
       <p>Create a function called <c>coursera_dict(soup)</c> that takes a soup object and returns a dictionary with Country as keys and Percentage as values. 
                        The soup object is created from the file 
                        'WikipediaMOOC-Jan-12-2018.html' which is a saved version of <q>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massive_open_online_course</q> from 2018.
                        Click on the following link to view the 
                            source: <url href="https://web.archive.org/web/20180112035358/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massive_open_online_course">link</url>
                                
                         For example, <c>coursera_dict(soup)</c>
                            should return a dictionary like: <c>{'United States': '27.7%', 'India': '8.8%', 'Brazil': '5.1%', ...}</c>.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="2">
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
        <cline>import requests</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1">
        <cline>def coursera_dict(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    coursera_enrollees = {}</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="13">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    tables = soup.find_all('table', class_='wikitable')</cline>
        </choice>
         <choice>
          <cline>    tables = soup.find('table', class_='wikitable') </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="12">
          <cline>    rows = tables[1].find_all('tr')</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
          <cline>    for row in rows[1:]:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
           <cline>          both_columns = row.find_all("td")</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="8">
           <cline>          key = both_columns[0].text</cline>
           <cline>          value = both_columns[1].text.rstrip("\n")</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
           <cline>          coursera_enrollees[key] = value</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="5">
           <cline>    return coursera_enrollees</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode4q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode4q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
     <p>Create a function called <c>keywords(soup)</c> that takes a soup object and returns a list of Python keywords.
                    The soup object is created from the file <c>PythonKeywords.html</c> which is a saved version of 
                    <q>https://www.w3schools.com/python/python_ref_keywords.asp</q> from 2026.
                       Click on the following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20260723003533/https://www.w3schools.com/python/python_ref_keywords.asp">link</url>.
                    For example, keywords(soup) should return a list that starts with: <c>['and', 'as', ...]</c>.
                 </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="6">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="2">
        <cline>def keywords(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    keywords = []</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="12">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    table = soup.find('table', class_="ws-table-all")</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    table = soup.find(table)</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
          <cline>    rows = table.find_all('tr')</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <choice>
          <cline>    for row in rows: </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    for row in rows[1:]:</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="14">
        <cline>        data = row.find("td")</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="11">
        <cline>        keyword = data.find('a').text</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="13">
        <cline>        keywords.append(keyword)</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="8">
        <cline>    return keywords</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode5q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode5q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
      <p>Create a function called <c>movies(soup)</c> that takes a soup object and returns a list of the top box office movies.
                    The soup object is created from the file <c>top-box-office-8-2026.html</c> which is a saved version of 
                    <q>https://www.imdb.com/chart/boxoffice/</q> from Aug 7-9th 2026.
                       Click on the following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20260812195232/https://www.imdb.com/chart/boxoffice/ ">link</url>.
                    For example, movies(soup) should return a list that starts with: <c>['Spider-Man: Brand New Day', 'The Odyssey', 'One Night Only', ...]</c>.
                 </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="4">
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
        <cline>import requests</cline>
      </block>
        <block order="2">
        <cline>def movies(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    movies = []</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1">
         <choice>
          <cline>    head_list = soup.find('h4', class_="ipc-title__text") </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    head_list = soup.find_all("h4", class_="ipc-title__text")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>    for header in head_list:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>        movies.append(header.text.strip())</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="10">
        <cline>    return movies</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode6q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode6q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
 <p>Write a function called <c>bsi_list(soup)</c> that takes in a parameter <c>soup</c> and returns a list of the section names and 
                    subsection names under Programs-Bachelor of Science in Information. 
                     The soup object is created from the file <c>BSI-Aug-3-2023.html</c> which is a saved version of 
                    <q>https://www.si.umich.edu/programs/bachelor-science-information</q> from Aug 3rd 2023.
                       Click on the following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20230803131935/https://www.si.umich.edu/programs/bachelor-science-information">link</url>.
                    For example, <c>bsi_list(soup)</c> should return a list that starts with: 
                 <c>['How do I apply?', 'Current U-M students', 'Transfer students', ...]</c>.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="5">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>def bsi_list(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    list1 = []</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1">
        <cline>    sections = soup.find_all('a', href = re.compile("/programs/bachelor-science-information/"))</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>    for section in sections[:-2]:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
        <choice>
          <cline>        list1.append(section.strip("\n").strip()) </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        list1.append(section.text.strip("\n").strip())</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>    return list1</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode7q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode7q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
       <p>Create a function called <c>bsi_dict(soup)</c> that takes in a parameter <c>soup</c> and returns a list of the section names and 
                    subsection names under Programs-Bachelor of Science in Information. 
                     The soup object is created from the file <c>BSI-Aug-3-2023.html</c> which is a saved version of 
                    <q>https://www.si.umich.edu/programs/bachelor-science-information</q> from Aug 3rd 2023.
                       Click on the following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20230803131935/https://www.si.umich.edu/programs/bachelor-science-information">link</url>.
                    For example, <c>bsi_list(soup)</c> should return a dictionary that starts with: 
                 <c>[</c>.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="7">
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>import re</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
        <cline>def bsi_dict(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    bsi_dict = {}</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    sections = soup.find_all('a', href = re.compile("/programs/bachelor-science-information/"))</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    sections = soup.find_all('link', href = re.compile("/programs/bachelor-science-information/")) </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>    for section in sections[:-2]:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
        <cline>        key = section.text.strip()</cline>
        <cline>        value = section.get('href')</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="2">
        <cline>        bsi_dict[bsi_list[key] = value</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="10">
        <cline>    return bsi_dict</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode8q_mu" label="bsoup_writecode8q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
      <p>Create a function called <c>envelope_address</c> that takes in a parameter <c>url</c> and uses BeautifulSoup to web scrape and return the address text in the footer as a list. For example, <c>envelope_address('https://www.si.umich.edu/programs/bachelor-science-information')</c>
            should return a list like: <c>['School of Information', 'University of Michigan', ...]</c>. Since we are scraping a live site the results may be different.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="1">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="11">
        <cline>def envelope_address(url):</cline>
        <cline>    out_list = []</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <choice>
          <cline>    address = soup.find('div', class = 'footer--address').text.strip() </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    address = soup.find('div', class_ = 'footer--address').text.strip()</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="5">
        <choice>
          <cline>    add_list = address.split() </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    add_list = address.split("\n")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
        <cline>    for add_line in add_list:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="12">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        out_list.append(add_line.strip())</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>        add_list.append(add_line.strip()) </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
        <cline>    return out_list</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode9q_mu"  label="bsoup_writecode9q_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
                    <p>Create a function called <c>name_email(soup)</c> that takes in a parameter <c>soup</c>, and returns a dictionary with the faculty names as keys and their email addresses as values.
                             The soup object is created from the file <c>FacultyUMSI-7-30-2025.html</c> which is a saved version of 
                    <q>https://www.si.umich.edu/people/directory/faculty/e</q> from July 30th 2025.
                       Click on the following link to view the source: <url href="http://web.archive.org/web/20250730015002/https://www.si.umich.edu/people/directory/faculty/e">link</url>.
                      For example, <c>name_email(souop)</c> should return <c>{'Paul Edwards': 'pne@umich.edu', 'Ron Eglash': 'eglash@umich.edu', ...'}</c>.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="1">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
          <cline>import requests</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>import re </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="8">
        <cline>def name_email(url):</cline>
        <cline>    names_list = []</cline>
        <cline>    emails_list = []</cline>
        <cline>    name_email_dict = {}</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <choice>
          <cline>    names = soup.find_all(class="research-person-profile__name") </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    names = soup.find_all(class_="research-person-profile__name")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="10">
        <choice>
          <cline>    emails = soup.find_all("link", target="_blank") </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    emails = soup.find_all("a", target="_blank")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
        <cline>    for name in names:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="5">
        <cline>        profile_name = name.text.strip().replace('\n \n ', '')</cline>
        <cline>        names_list.append(profile_name)</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6">
        <cline>    for email in emails:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
        <cline>        profile_email = email.get("href")</cline>
        <cline>        profile_email = profile_email.replace('mailto:', '')</cline>
        <cline>        emails_list.append(profile_email)</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="14">
        <cline>    for i in range(len(names_list)):</cline>
        <cline>        name_email_dict[names_list[i]] = emails_list[i]</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>    return name_email_dict</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
  <activity xml:id="bsoup_writecode10qv2_mu" label="bsoup_writecode10qv2_mu" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide" language="python">
    <statement>
      <p>Create a function called <c>program_email</c> that takes in a parameter <c>url</c>, uses BeautifulSoup to web scrape the program names under Email Addresses
            and Admissions (BSI program, MSI program, etc.) and their associated email addresses, and returns a dictionary with the program names as keys and the email addresses as values.
            For example, <c>program_email('https://www.si.umich.edu/about-umsi/contact-us')</c> should return something like <c>{'BSI program': 'umsi.undergrad@umich.edu', 'MSI program': 'umsi.admissions@umich.edu', ...}</c>. Since we are scraping a live site the results may be different.</p>
    </statement>
    <blocks numbered="left">
      <block order="8">
        <cline>import requests</cline>
        <cline>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="12">
        <cline>def program_email(soup):</cline>
        <cline>    program_email_dict = {}</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1">
        <cline>    programs_section = soup.find("ul", dir="ltr")</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="9">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    programs = programs_section.find_all("li")</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    programs = programs_section.find("li") </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="5">
        <cline>    for program in programs:</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="13">
        <cline>        split_name_email = program.text.split(":")</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7">
        <cline>        program_name = split_name_email[0]</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="4">
        <choice>
          <cline>        program_email = split_name_email[0].replace("\xa0", "") </cline>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>        program_email = split_name_email[1].replace("\xa0", "")</cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block order="3">
        <cline>        program_email_dict[program_name] = program_email</cline>
      </block>
      <block order="14">
        <cline>    return program_email_dict</cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
</section>
